---
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { consent } from '../copy'
import { pages, site } from '../site'
import '../styles/home.css'

/* The privacy notice Microsoft Clarity's terms require: who runs the site, what is
   recorded, who else gets it, the cookies, and how to change your answer. */
const updated = pages.privacy.modified
const updatedLabel = new Date(updated).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' })
---

<BaseLayout page={pages.privacy} title="Privacy. WCGW" description="What wcgw.fun records, and what it doesn't.">
  <div class="page">
    <header class="masthead">
      <a class="home" href="/" aria-label="WCGW home"><Logo /></a>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="act row privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy">
        <p class="gutter">Privacy</p>
        <div class="body">
          <h1 id="privacy">What this site records</h1>
          <p>
            wcgw.fun is run by WCGW, the studio of Radu Ceucă in Bucharest. It sells no ads and keeps no
            accounts. It does watch how the page is used, to see what goes wrong.
          </p>

          <h2>What gets recorded</h2>
          <p>
            The site uses <a class="link" href="https://clarity.microsoft.com" rel="noopener">Microsoft Clarity</a>.
            It records where you click, how far you scroll and how the mouse moves, which pages you open, and
            your browser, device and country. Visits can be replayed as a recording. This starts on your first
            visit, without cookies.
          </p>

          <h2>Cookies</h2>
          <p>
            None until you say yes. After a yes, Clarity sets two on this site, <code>_clck</code> and
            <code>_clsk</code>, to tie your pages and visits together. Microsoft may also set its own, such as
            <code>MUID</code> and <code>CLID</code>, on its domains
            (<a class="link" href="https://learn.microsoft.com/clarity/setup-and-installation/clarity-cookies" rel="noopener">the full list</a>).
            A no sets none. Advertising use is refused either way.
          </p>

          <h2>Who sees it</h2>
          <p>
            Radu, in the Clarity dashboard. Microsoft collects and processes the data too, as its
            <a class="link" href="https://www.microsoft.com/privacy/privacystatement" rel="noopener">privacy statement</a>
            describes. WCGW doesn’t sell it or use it for ads.
          </p>

          <h2>Why</h2>
          <p>
            The cookieless recording rests on WCGW’s interest in a site that works. The cookies rest on your
            yes.
          </p>

          <h2>On your device</h2>
          <p>Your theme and your cookie answer stay in your browser’s storage. They are never sent anywhere.</p>

          <h2>Changing your mind</h2>
          <p>
            Use <em>Cookies</em> at the bottom of any page. Clarity also honours Global Privacy Control. To see
            or delete what was recorded about you, write to
            <a class="link" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
          </p>

          <p class="updated">Updated <time datetime={updated}>{updatedLabel}</time>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="row">
      <div class="body foot">
        <p>© <span id="year">2026</span> WCGW, the studio of Radu Ceucă.</p>
        {site.clarityId && <p><button class="link" type="button" data-consent-open>{consent.reopen}</button></p>}
      </div>
    </footer>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .home {
    display: block;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .home:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--pencil);
    outline-offset: 0.2em;
  }
  .privacy { margin-top: clamp(4.5rem, 3rem + 7vw, 8.75rem); }
  h1 { margin-top: 0; font-size: inherit; letter-spacing: 0; line-height: inherit; }
  h2 { font-size: inherit; color: var(--soft); }
  h2 + p { margin-top: 0.2em; }
  code { font: inherit; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.85em; }
  .updated { color: var(--soft); }
</style>
